<<~ ahu #rule >>

!! Extracted Rule

Under an invoked stance, register measures confidence within that standpoint's evaluation frame; register does not measure universal truth-weight. The coupling rides the lens --- absent an invoked `syad` lens, a confidence number reads through all five stances at once (the full field, quiet stances named --- the default equals a parameterless `<<~ syad >>`); invoking specific stances narrows the measure to those named.

The active stance names the `syād` --- the standpoint from which the number reads. The sigil performs the //syāt// office: it licenses a stance's claim, the //naya// wing licensing a //saptabhaṅgī// predication (canon: `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/the-syad-perspectives#relationship`).

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #stance-array >>

!! Stance Array Extraction

The canon transition closed on one point:
all five stances surface at all times.

No live form drops positions.
A quiet stance stays encoded as quiet, not missing.

The order stays fixed:
Philosopher.
Poet.
Satirist.
Humorist.
Private.

The array therefore shows both load and restraint.
Combination reads through simultaneous flags, not omission.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #reading-question >>

!! Reading Question

When you read a register value,
ask one question first:
what does this number measure for this stance?

The answer changes with the stance.
The number does not.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #spectrum-drift >>

!! Spectrum Drift

Some archive bridges described Sri Syadasti as one truth-value spectrum.
That wording helped the transition,
but it does not hold the live fold cleanly.

The canon reading does not flatten all stances into one axis.
It keeps distinct measures per stance,
and it keeps `avaktavya` from collapsing into mere uncertainty.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #saptabhangi >>

!! Saptabhangi Extraction

Sri Syadasti's catma reproduces the Jaina Saptabhangi:
seven predicates generated from three primitives.

| # | Sanskrit | English | Primitives |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | syād-asti | perhaps true | T |
| 2 | syād-nāsti | perhaps false | F |
| 3 | syād-asti-nāsti | perhaps true and false (in turn, krama) | T + F |
| 4 | syād-avaktavya | perhaps inexpressible (the pole, yugapad) | M |
| 5 | syād-asti-avaktavya | perhaps true and inexpressible | T + M |
| 6 | syād-nāsti-avaktavya | perhaps false and inexpressible | F + M |
| 7 | syād-asti-nāsti-avaktavya | perhaps true, false, and inexpressible | T + F + M |

`avaktavya` does not name ignorance.
It names a frame where the true/false axis does not apply cleanly.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #multi-stance-boundary >>

!! Multi-Stance Boundary

Some archive readings described multi-stance register as a center or gesture toward center.
That bridge does not govern the live fold.

The live fold keeps register as a point.
The visible stance array carries the spread.
Combination reads through simultaneous measures,
not through a collapsed average.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #canon-transition >>

!! Canon Transition

The archive records one explicit correction event:
a compact surface that showed only selected stances got named as a bug.

From that turn forward,
all five positions stayed mandatory.
The correction closed omission-based live examples.

<<~/ahu >>

<<~ ahu #legacy-notes >>

!! Legacy Notes

These readings stay archive-only now:

* partial-emission stance strings
* "stance-count is fuzz"
* compact stance codepaths
* open-ended modifier vocabularies without a fixed array

They remain useful as lineage.
They do not govern the live fold.

